The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions and the statements of its President concerning the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC), in particular resolutions 2717 (2023), 2746 (2024), and 2765 (2024),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity, and territorial integrity of the DRC and of all States in the region, and emphasizing that any violation thereof, including through external military intervention or the illegal presence of foreign troops, is contrary to the Charter of the United Nations,

Expressing its grave alarm at the rapid escalation of violence and the offensive launched by the Mouvement du 23 mars (M23) in North Kivu and South Kivu, including the advance towards Goma, the capture of Minova and surrounding areas, and the direct threat posed to urban centres, which has triggered massive civilian displacement and exacerbated an already catastrophic humanitarian crisis,

Reiterating its condemnation of all foreign military support provided to the M23 or to any other armed group operating in the DRC, as documented in the reports of the Group of Experts on the DRC, and deeply concerned by the corroborated presence and direct involvement of the Rwandan Defence Forces (RDF) on Congolese territory supporting the M23,

Condemning equally any military cooperation with or support provided by any forces, including elements of the Armed Forces of the Democratic Republic of the Congo (FARDC), to armed groups, in particular the Democratic Forces for the Liberation of Rwanda (FDLR), and reiterating that all such collaboration must cease immediately,

Strongly condemning all attacks against civilians, sexual and gender-based violence, violations of international humanitarian law, and attacks directed against personnel and installations of the United Nations Organization Stabilization Mission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (MONUSCO) and the Southern African Development Community Mission in the Democratic Republic of Congo (SAMIDRC), which may constitute war crimes,

Reaffirming the crucial importance of the regional peace efforts, notably the Luanda and Nairobi processes, and deeply regretting the breakdown of the ceasefire and the suspension of diplomatic relations between the DRC and Rwanda,

Reiterating that the illegal exploitation and trade of natural resources, including coltan from mining areas in North Kivu, continues to fuel armed conflict and finance illicit armed groups in eastern DRC,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the military offensive and territorial expansion of the M23 in North Kivu and South Kivu, and demands that the M23 immediately cease all hostilities, halt its advance, and withdraw completely and unconditionally from all occupied territories, in accordance with the decisions taken within the framework of the Luanda and Nairobi processes;

2. Demands the immediate cessation of all external support to the M23 and any other armed group operating in the DRC, and explicitly calls upon the Government of Rwanda to immediately withdraw all its military personnel, military assets, and surface-to-air missile systems from the territory of the DRC;

3. Calls upon the Government of the DRC to immediately put an end to any cooperation, coordination, or provision of support by the FARDC to armed groups, notably the FDLR and local armed groups (Wazalendo), and to implement without delay the FDLR neutralization plan agreed under the Luanda process;

4. Urges all armed groups operating in eastern DRC, including the M23, FDLR, Allied Democratic Forces (ADF), and CODECO, to immediately lay down their arms, cease all violence against civilians, and unconditionally enter into the national Disarmament, Demobilization, Community Recovery and Stabilization Program (P-DDRCS);

5. Demands that all parties respect international humanitarian law, ensure the protection of civilians, allow safe, rapid, and unhindered humanitarian access to all persons in need, and cease any attacks on internally displaced persons’ camps;

6. Reaffirms its full support for MONUSCO and SAMIDRC in the execution of their mandates, stresses the imperative of protecting peacekeepers, demands that all parties cease any obstruction or intimidation against United Nations and regional personnel, and reiterates its readiness to adopt targeted sanctions against individuals and entities threatening the peace, security, or stability of the DRC, including those attacking peacekeepers;

7. Calls upon the DRC and Rwanda to urgently de-escalate tensions, restore diplomatic dialogue, and re-engage in good faith under the mediation of Angola within the framework of the Luanda process;

8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

